-- title:
--   Norm approximation at finitely many points from pointwise weak closures
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals.
--
--   1. **Finite norm evaluation.** For arguments $a_1,\dots,a_m$, the linear map $f\mapsto(f(a_1),\dots,f(a_m))\in X^m$. It is continuous into the weak topology of $X^m$.
--   2. **Simultaneous approximation.** If $s$ is a convex family of weak-valued maps and $f$ lies in its pointwise weak closure, then for every finite family of arguments and $\varepsilon>0$ some $g\in s$ satisfies
--   $$
--   \|g(a_i)-f(a_i)\|<\varepsilon\quad\text{for all }i .
--   $$
--   This holds because convex sets of $X^m$ have the same weak and norm closures.
--   3. **Converse.** Simultaneous finite approximation in a pseudometric codomain implies membership in the pointwise closure.
